Windows release handoff — installed-brand validator correction

Scope
- Windows-only follow-up to f399e882. No Tauri, icon asset, NSIS hook, application runtime, macOS, or Linux behavior changed.
- Release v1.1.5 remains blocked until this commit passes the full PR CI and review. Do not create or move a release tag from this note alone.

Observed root cause
- The NSIS package built successfully and installed successfully.
- ProductName=Beaver and FileVersion/ProductVersion=1.1.5 were correct.
- The installed validator failed as installed-brand-icon-size under PowerShell 7 and installed-brand-icon-content under Windows PowerShell 5.1.
- System.Drawing.Icon.ExtractAssociatedIcon returned 40x40 at Windows DPI 125%, while the reference decoder returned 32x32.
- The managed ICO decoder also rendered different pixels across the two PowerShell/.NET engines.
- A native diagnostic using PrivateExtractIcons at explicit 32x32 produced identical EXE/ICO hashes under both engines. The package and embedded icon were correct; the validator was the false negative.

Correction
- Added scripts/release/windows-native-icon.ps1 as the single owner of fixed-size native Windows icon extraction.
- Both the installed EXE and source ICO now use PrivateExtractIcons at exactly 32x32.
- Native HICON handles and borrowed/cloned Drawing.Icon objects are released on every path; DestroyIcon failure is propagated.
- The two-background visible-pixel comparison remains unchanged.
- The native module is separate so every code/test file remains below the 230-line project limit.

Regression coverage (TDD)
- Before implementation, package-migration.test.mjs failed because the fixed native extractor was absent.
- Before implementation, check-nsis-migration.test.ps1 failed under both pwsh and Windows PowerShell because Get-FixedSizeNativeIcon did not exist.
- The tests now exercise the real repository ICO twice at 32x32, compare deterministic black/white rendered hashes, require native extraction/cleanup, and prevent reintroduction of the two DPI/runtime-dependent APIs in the installed-brand validator.

